text:
mika: quick q — for internal inference endpoints, are we leaning toward gRPC streaming as the canonical contract and doing JSON transcoding at the gateway for browser clients, or keep REST/JSON primary and treat gRPC as opt-in?

samir: +1 for gRPC streaming as primary for service-to-service. Less CPU per-request, native backpressure, bidi streams for websockets/long-running evals. Token-level flow control feels cleaner there.

jess: worried about the gateway surface. Browsers -> gRPC-web or gRPC-transcode adds complexity. Also ops: if the gateway has to stitch streams into chunked JSON/SSE, we need to maintain per-token correlation and quotas — doable but not trivial.

linh: what about hybrid: internal services talk gRPC streaming, gateway exposes two HTTP shapes: 1) JSON chunked SSE for browsers 2) a plain JSON REST for sync calls. Gateway handles token -> chunk mapping and emits token metrics. That keeps clients simple.

samir: that's basically what I meant. two concerns: (a) Envoy gRPC-web implementation strips some flow-control semantics, (b) token-level quotas need to be enforced either at the gateway or at the runtime — doing it at both is tricky.

jess: for (a) we could use an ingress sidecar specifically for inference routes that understands streaming semantics, rather than vanilla envoy. For (b) we should prefer enforcement at the runtime for correctness, and have gateway do optimistic quota checks (fast-fail if over threshold) and emit telemetry for billing/alerts.

mika: good. ops question: when we do schema changes to the streaming proto (e.g., add optional chunk_type or metadata envelope) how do we roll that safely without breaking the gateway mapping or browser clients?

linh: recommend a stable "stream_envelope" minimal proto with a oneof { token_chunk, metadata, control } and keep additions inside nested extension map. That way gateway mapping to JSON keeps stable keys. Example proto snippet:

```proto
message StreamEnvelope {
  oneof payload {
    TokenChunk token = 1;
    StreamMeta meta = 2;
    ControlSignal ctrl = 3;
  }
}
message TokenChunk {
  bytes token_bytes = 1;
  uint32 seq = 2;
}
```

samir: nice — versioning strategy: add new enum values or new optional fields, never repurpose tags. Gateway does best-effort mapping of unknown fields to an _extra map so JSON clients don't lose info.

jess: for rollout UX: suggest header-driven canary first. Have console set X-Use-GRPC: true for 0/5/25/50% routes and we adjust weight in router. Also add a health probe that validates token-level metrics and delta on tail latency before promoting.

infra-bot: deploy scheduled: canary proxy image v2026.03.21-ggw -> 10% at 1743219900 (notified).

mika: action items: 1) jess + linh to spec gateway transcode behavior and unknown-field mapping (PR by Fri) 2) samir to draft runtime quota enforcement doc (token semantics) 3) I'll wire console flags for header canary and add rollout dashboard card.

jess: I'll prototype an envoy filter but would prefer a small dedicated sidecar if perf tests show cpu is high.

samir: perf test plan: compare throughput gRPC-streaming -> runtime vs json-chunked gateway -> runtime with 1k concurrent streams. I'll run baseline tomorrow.

mika: sounds good. :thumbsup:

linh: one more: telemetry — please include per-token hashing id (not PII) so we can stitch across gateway/runtime for debug. use header X-Token-Span or in meta envelope.

jess: agreed. add sampling flag in header to avoid high cardinality.

mika: thanks all — if no blocker we'll start canary next week after PRs land.
